<name><surname>Daly</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Temporal trends of invasive disease due to <italic>Streptococcus pneumoniae</italic> among children in the intermountain west: emergence of nonvaccine serogroups.</article-title><source>Clin Infect Dis</source><volume>41</volume><fpage>21</fpage><lpage>29</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15937758</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Shah1"><label>6</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shah</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name><name><surname>Ratner</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Trends in pneumococcal disease-associated hospitalizations.</article-title><source>Clin Infect Dis</source><volume>42</volume><fpage>e1</fpage><lpage>5</lpage><comment>Epub 2005 Nov 23</comment><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16323082</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Messina1"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Messina</surname><given-names>AF</given-names></name><name><surname>Katz-Gaynor</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Barton</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Ahmad</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Ghaffar</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Impact of pneumococcal conjugate vaccine on serotype distribution and antimicrobial resistance of invasive <italic>Streptococcus pneumoniae</italic> isolates in Dallas, TX, children from 1999 through 2005.</article-title><source>Pediatr Infect Dis J</source><volume>26</volume><fpage>461</fpage><lpage>467</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17529859</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Steenhoff1"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Steenhoff</surname><given-names>AP</given-names></name><name><surname>Shah</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name><name><surname>Ratner</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Patil</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>McGowan</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Emergence of vaccine-related pneumococcal serotypes as a cause of bacteremia.</article-title><source>Clin Infect Dis</source><volume>42</volume><fpage>907</fpage><lpage>914</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16511752</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Clinical1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="book"><collab>Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI)</collab><year>2008</year><article-title>Performance standards for antimicrobial susceptibility testing.</article-title><publisher-loc>Wayne, PA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Clinical Laboratory and Standards Institute</publisher-name><comment>M100-S18.</comment></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-National1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="book"><collab>National Committee for Clinical Laboratory Standards</collab><year>2007</year><article-title>Performance standards for antimicrobial susceptibility testing.</article-title><publisher-loc>Wayne, PA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Clinical Laboratory and Standards Institute</publisher-name><comment>Volume 27 #1 M100-S17.</comment></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Singleton1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Singleton</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Hennessy</surname><given-names>TW</given-names></name><name><surname>Bulkow</surname><given-names>LR</given-names></name><name><surname>Hammitt</surname><given-names>LL</given-names></name><name><surname>Zulz</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Invasive pneumococcal disease caused by nonvaccine serotypes among Alaska native children with high levels of 7-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine coverage.</article-title><source>JAMA</source><volume>297</volume><fpage>1784</fpage><lpage>1792</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17456820</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Jacobs1"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jacobs</surname><given-names>MR</given-names></name><name><surname>Good</surname><given-names>CE</given-names></name><name><surname>Beall</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Bajaksouzian</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Windau</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>Changes in serotype and antimicrobial susceptibility of invasive <italic>Streptococcus pneumoniae</italic> strains in Cleveland: a quarter century of experience.</article-title><source>J Clin Microbiol</source><volume>46</volume><fpage>982</fpage><lpage>990</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18234877</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005965-Poehling1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Poehling</surname><given-names>KA</given-names></name><name><surname>Talbot</surname><given-names>TR</given-names></name><name><surname>Griffin</surname><given-names>MR</given-names></name><name><surname>Craig</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Whitney</surname><given-names>CG</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Invasive pneumococcal disease among infants before and after introduction of pneumococcal conjugate vaccine.</article-title><source>JAMA</source><volume>295</volume><fpage>1668</fpage><lpage>1674</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16609088</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests: </bold>As indicated in the acknowledgements section, the authors had helpful conversations with Dr. Adam Ratner, who is also in the same pediatric infectious diseases division as Dr. Lisa Saiman and Ann Smith.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding: </bold>The authors have no support or funding to report.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>